The docstring-staleness playbook told the next reader three things that the
2026-09-04 run found to be wrong or incomplete. Correcting them where they are
loaded, not only in a notebook entry.

1. THE ipc.py ARTIFACT EXPLAINS WHY A FILE APPEARS, NOT WHETHER IT DRIFTED.
   The playbook records that a previous audit's own function-docstring fix
   re-flags a file, and says to check whether that produced the flag. True, but
   read as licence to skip it: `linkers/ipc.py` re-flagged AND carried four real
   module-docstring drifts, the largest a promise the code never keeps --
   `worker.postMessage(data) -> event_publishes` while POSTMESSAGE_PATTERN sets
   an empty channel and the edge loop skips it, so no edge and no symbol are
   ever emitted. Skipping on the artifact would have missed it twice running.

2. RUF002 HAS A COMMENT TWIN, RUF003. The playbook warns about the ambiguous
   multiplication sign in docstrings. The same gate fires on comments: this run
   tripped RUF003 on a set-union sign in a new axis_meta_keys.py comment.

3. A THIRD FAMILY THE SCAN CANNOT SEE: file:line CITATION ROT. A registry that
   cites source it does not own by line number rots whenever ANY line is
   inserted above the cited one -- no docstring edit, no blame-date change, so
   no arm of check-docstring-drift can see it, exactly as with retired
   vocabulary. It bit twice in one day: three citations from real code movement,
   and a fourth from a pure module-docstring edit to cpp.py in another package.
   The first breakage sat red on dev for a day because no test-selection arm
   runs the guarding test when the CITED file changes. Added the one-loop check
   to run after any bundle that changes line counts, and the INV-fogul pointer.

Also adds the 2026-09-04 row to the calibration table (24 flagged / 18 real /
75%) and its notebook pointer. The row is worth reading with the caveat the
playbook already carries: the registry pass found drift in 7 files the scan
flagged none of, so the flagged count measures the scan, not the tree.

